    "text_full": "Skip to content\nAbout Us\nPrograms\nNews & Media\nDONATE\nFR\nHERITAGE MINUTES\nPODCASTS\nVIDEOS\nEDUCATION GUIDES\nSEARCH PRODUCTIONS\nHome\n / Podcasts / A Place to Belong\nA Place to Belong\n\nIn 1971, Canada became the first country in the world to adopt an official multiculturalism policy. It was meant to preserve cultural freedoms and recognize the contributions of diverse groups to Canadian society. Today it’s a defining feature of the Canadian identity. But for much of our history, that wasn’t the case. We explore the reasons why in this five-part series, A Place to Belong: A History of Multiculturalism in Canada, produced by Historica Canada and made possible in part by the Government of Canada.\n\nFeatures\nA Place to Belong: Project Neighbourhood (Episode 5)\nFeatured\nIn this episode, we asked: What does the multiculturalism policy look like in practice?\nA Place to Belong: Mayor of Toronto’s Chinatown (Episode 3)\nFeatured\nToronto’s Chinatown – one of the largest in North America – is filled with hustle and bustle.\nA Place to Belong: Hogan's Alley (Episode 4)\nFeatured\nIn the late 1960s, Hogan’s Alley was the only area in Vancouver with a largely Black population, mainly because of the housing discrimination pervasive in the city.\nAll (5) Episodes\nSort by:\nDate\nA to Z\nSearch & Filter\nA Place to Belong: Project Neighbourhood (Episode 5)\n2021\nA Place to Belong: Hogan's Alley (Episode 4)\n2021\nA Place to Belong: Mayor of Toronto’s Chinatown (Episode 3)\n2021\nA Place to Belong: Battle of the Hatpins (Episode 2)\n2021\nA Place to Belong: How We Got Here (Episode 1)\n2021\n\nHistorica Canada is a charitable organization that offers programs in both official languages that you can use to explore, learn, reflect on our history, and consider what it means to be Canadian.\n\nHistorica Canada's business number is 885434720RR0001\n\nAbout Us\nPolicies\nCareers\nHistorica Canada Content Usage Request Form\n\nFollow Us\nMore From Historica Canada\nCitizenship Challenge\nHeritage Minutes\nThe Memory Project\nThe Canadian Encyclopedia\nPartnership: The World Remembers\n© Historica Canada 2026. All rights reserved.\nWebsite by DS"
